WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.056 --> 00:00:13.715
When Laura Ingalls’ older sister, Mary, became ill with scarlet fever and lost her sight, their father told 12-year-old Laura that she must become

2
00:00:13.716 --> 00:00:24.124
Mary’s link to light and color in her newly darkened world. Laura described a bustling world of railroad men building tracks across the prairie

3
00:00:24.125 --> 00:00:36.278
and the people who sat inside the trains on soft, red-velvet seats. Little did anyone know that tomboyish Laura would later do the same

4
00:00:36.279 --> 00:00:52.016
for other children in a series of popular books, painting pictures with words of her life as a pioneer girl. Laura Ingalls was born

5
00:00:52.017 --> 00:01:03.767
on February 7, 1867, near the town of Pepin, Wisconsin, to Charles and Caroline Ingalls (known as "Pa" and "Ma" in her books), and

6
00:01:03.768 --> 00:01:16.767
lived in the woods in a log cabin. Mountain lions, bears, and wolves roamed freely in the woods as the family gathered around a fire

7
00:01:16.768 --> 00:01:29.415
each night to listen to Ma read and Pa play his fiddle. Pa hunted and farmed, while Ma kept house. When Pa heard that

8
00:01:29.416 --> 00:01:44.550
Kansas Territory was opening to settlers, he relished the idea of farming on the open and treeless prairie. So in 1868, when Mary and

9
00:01:44.551 --> 00:01:54.051
Laura were both toddlers, he sold the Wisconsin farm, and the family headed almost six hundred miles southwest to Kansas. In their horse-drawn covered

10
00:01:54.052 --> 00:01:59.861
wagon, the family crossed the ice on the Upper Mississippi River into Minnesota, and then turned south through Iowa and Missouri, crossing the Missouri

11
00:01:59.862 --> 00:02:06.939
River with their wagon on a raft. Eventually, the Ingalls arrived in southeastern Kansas just outside the new town of Independence and set up

12
00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:17.458
housekeeping. But it wasn’t long before they realized that they had settled on Osage Indian lands. At that time, many whites had been encouraged

13
00:02:17.459 --> 00:02:24.434
to move to Kansas to settle on these lands illegally. The members of the Osage tribe were angry at the newcomers, and U.S. soldiers

14
00:02:24.435 --> 00:02:32.195
were sent to Kansas to deal with the resentment of both the Indians and the settlers. After only a year, the Ingalls decided to

15
00:02:32.196 --> 00:02:42.840
leave their prairie home and go back to Wisconsin. When Ingalls was seven, her father became restless again, so the family settled in Walnut

16
00:02:42.841 --> 00:02:48.842
Grove, Minnesota. Life was busy for Laura Ingalls as she helped churn butter, sew quilts, and wash dishes, but she was curious about the natural

17
00:02:48.843 --> 00:02:56.321
world around her too. She enjoyed playing outside in the sunshine with her sister and the family’s bulldog, Jack. But she also loved listening

18
00:02:56.322 --> 00:03:06.410
to her mother read, which sparked her interest in expressing herself through words. However, along with the sunshine came the harsh Minnesota winters, when

19
00:03:06.411 --> 00:03:20.071
blizzards blasted snow and sleet against the house for days. One summer, swarms of grasshoppers descended onto the family’s wheat fields like huge, dark

20
00:03:20.072 --> 00:03:29.899
clouds and destroyed the crop, so Pa had to leave the family to work two hundred miles away. Laura Ingalls pitched in to help

21
00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:35.551
with chores while Ma took care of the homestead and a new baby named Freddie, who died at only nine months old. The family

22
00:03:35.552 --> 00:03:44.440
missed Pa as they struggled to keep going under these difficult conditions. In 1879, the family left Minnesota and became one of the first

23
00:03:44.441 --> 00:03:50.129
settlers in the new town of De Smet, South Dakota. As other settlers arrived, the Ingalls watched the population swell around them. Over the

24
00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:57.709
years, the Ingalls family had also grown to include two more girls, Carrie and Grace. Because of the family’s frequent moves, Laura Ingalls was

25
00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:13.191
mostly self-taught, but at 15 years old, she received a teaching certificate and began teaching at a one-room schoolhouse about 12 miles away from

26
00:04:13.192 --> 00:04:24.688
her home. During the week, Ingalls boarded with a family near the school, and many times, a kind neighbor named Almanzo Wilder would drive her

27
00:04:24.689 --> 00:04:30.274
home for weekend visits with her family. After two years of driving back and forth, they fell in love and got married. The Wilders

28
00:04:30.275 --> 00:04:39.172
settled down to farm the land, and at 19 years old, Laura Ingalls Wilder gave birth to a daughter, Rose. Many of the hardships

29
00:04:39.173 --> 00:04:45.981
Ingalls Wilder faced as a child were repeated in her life with her husband. Hail destroyed their wheat crop, their barn burned down, and

30
00:04:45.982 --> 00:04:55.082
her husband was left disabled when diphtheria struck the family. In 1889, Laura Ingalls Wilder gave birth to a son who died days later,

31
00:04:55.083 --> 00:05:05.567
and shortly after that, the family home burned down. Laura Ingalls Wilder’s earlier experiences gave her the courage she needed to endure&#8212;nothing hindered her

32
00:05:05.568 --> 00:05:14.889
spirit. So on July 17, 1894, the Wilder family decided to journey to Mansfield, Missouri, and Laura Ingalls Wilder kept a journal of their

33
00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:28.052
travels to their new home. She sent the account of her journey back to the De Smet News , her old hometown newspaper, and

34
00:05:28.053 --> 00:05:37.783
it became her first published story. The family built a farm they called Rocky Ridge, and Ingalls Wilder sold wood she chopped herself and

35
00:05:37.784 --> 00:05:53.205
took a job sewing buttonholes for a dressmaker 12 hours a day, 6 days a week, to keep the family financially afloat. But at

36
00:05:53.206 --> 00:06:07.345
age 44, Laura Ingalls Wilder embarked on a writing career that began with a 12-year assignment as a columnist and editor for the Missouri

37
00:06:07.346 --> 00:06:18.089
Ruralist , a farming publication. She based a lot of her writing on her childhood memories, daily experiences on the farm, and any subject her

38
00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:25.164
lively mind became interested in. Soon, she was contributing articles to McCall’s Magazine and the St. Louis Star . This nonfiction writing helped Laura

39
00:06:25.165 --> 00:06:35.219
Ingalls Wilder learn about a vast array of subjects and develop a fierce, questioning mind. Even though the options for women in rural Missouri

40
00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:48.979
were severely limited at the time, outspoken Ingalls Wilder consistently challenged traditional ideas of what women should think and do. Her daughter, Rose, who

41
00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:58.736
became a writer too, had grown up listening to and being inspired by the stories her mother told of life in the wilderness. She

42
00:06:58.737 --> 00:07:03.712
encouraged her mother to write for children about her pioneer childhood. Ingalls Wilder agreed and said that her experiences were "altogether too good to

43
00:07:03.713 --> 00:07:11.120
be lost." So in 1932, when she was 65, Laura Ingalls Wilder started a series of seven historical children’s novels called the "Little House"

44
00:07:11.121 --> 00:07:26.943
books, beginning with Little House in the Big Woods . The book featured an adventurous Pa, a patient Ma, and their four daughters: Mary,

45
00:07:26.944 --> 00:07:39.016
Laura, Carrie, and Grace, and it became an instant bestseller. "The Little House books are stories of long ago," Laura said in a letter

46
00:07:39.017 --> 00:07:45.112
to her readers. "So many changes have made living and learning easier. But the real things haven’t changed. It is still best to be

47
00:07:45.113 --> 00:07:52.157
honest and truthful; to make the most of what we have; to be happy with simple pleasures and to be cheerful and have courage when

48
00:07:52.158 --> 00:08:04.682
things go wrong." On February 10, 1957, Laura Ingalls Wilder died at Rocky Ridge at the age of ninety. She left behind a series

49
00:08:04.683 --> 00:08:12.335
of books that portray the western pioneer woman as someone with ingenuity, perseverance, and endurance, in a way that is rooted in real life.

50
00:08:12.336 --> 00:08:24.821
When she wrote that "Laura . . . could never get enough sunshine soaked into her bones" in Little Town on the Prairie ,

51
00:08:24.822 --> 00:08:39.560
she expressed the feeling of being a child in a wild place with all the freedom in the world. Although the life that Laura

52
00:08:39.561 --> 00:08:51.526
Ingalls Wilder wrote about has largely disappeared, it lives on in her books, which continue to sell hundreds of thousands of copies every year.

